  "worked_solution": "First, we let $f(x)={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9.$ \n\nSince $f(x)$ has real coefficients and ${x}_{1}=-3\\text{i}$ is a root of $f(x),$ the complex conjugate \n\n$$\n\\overset{\u2015}{{x}_{1}}=\\overset{\u2015}{-3\\text{i}}=3\\text{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root of $f(x).$\n\nNow, according to the factor theorem, \n$(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i}))$\nis a factor of $f(x).$ Expanding the parentheses, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3\\text{i})(x-(-3\\text{i})) & ={x}^{2}+(3\\text{i})(-3\\text{i}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the quotient when $f(x)$ is divided by ${x}^{2}+9$ using polynomial division.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & {x}^{2}+9\\,\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+9x-9\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{x}^{3}+9x}_{\u2015} \\\\  & -{x}^{2}\\,\\,-9 \\\\  & \\,{-\\,{x}^{2}\\,\\,-9}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nThus, $f(x)=({x}^{2}+9)(x-1).$ \n\nTo find the remaining root, we set the linear factor equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots are $x=1,\\pm 3\\text{i}.$",
